{%- comment -%}
  Lives on the same shared default page template as "PM: About" and
  "PM: Press" (pm-about.liquid / pm-press.liquid) -- the page.handle
  check below is what keeps this section's content scoped to just
  that one page.

  The page's real handle turned out to be
  "email-preferences-tracking-off", not "email-preferences" -- Admin
  doesn't support a real ?query=string in a page handle, so setting it
  to "email-preferences?tracking=off" just got slugified into one
  handle, folding "?tracking=off" into the path itself rather than
  making it an actual URL query parameter. `contains` instead of an
  exact match keeps this working even if the handle changes again.

  Purely a static confirmation screen: customers land here after
  clicking "Disable tracking" in a Klaviyo email, and the click itself
  is already recorded by Klaviyo before they ever reach this page.
  Deliberately has no form, no button that submits anything, no
  script, and no tracking of any kind -- just a plain visual
  confirmation.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if page.handle contains 'email-preferences' -%}
{{ 'pm-homepage.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'pm-email-preferences.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

<div class="pm-homepage">
  <section class="pm-email-pref">
    <h1 class="pm-serif pm-email-pref-title">Preference saved</h1>
    <p class="pm-email-pref-body">Thank you, your preference has been recorded. You will continue to receive all our communications, but without any tracking of opens or clicked links.</p>
    <p class="pm-email-pref-note">You can change this choice at any time by contacting us.</p>
    <a href="/" class="pm-link">Return to the shop</a>
  </section>
</div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "PM: Email preferences",
  "tag": "section"
}
{% endschema %}
